1. Introduction

1.1 From model selection to topology selection

Conventional machine learning assumes a fixed architecture. Modern heterogeneous AI systems violate this assumption: they route subtasks to language models, vision models, symbolic engines, retrieval systems, simulators, verifiers, external APIs, and human operators, and the pattern of routing may change as tasks evolve.

Represent such a system as a labeled directed graph  G = (V, E, \xi, \omega, \rho) . The relevant decision is no longer only which model is best? but:

Which computational topology is best for the current task, and is it worth the cost of changing to it?

1.2 Terminology

We avoid the phrase computational topology in the mathematical sense of algorithmic topology (persistent homology, etc.). Throughout this document, computational topology means a labeled directed graph of heterogeneous computational agents. Where ambiguity might arise, we write computational graph topology.

1.3 Contributions of this draft

1. Formal definitions of topology space, augmented state space, task state, and utility (§4–§6).
2. A directed quasi-pseudometric theory of transition costs with proofs of the triangle inequality under memoryless admissibility (§11–§12).
3. A static switching theorem with proof (§13).
4. A hysteresis band characterization with asymmetric costs (§14).
5. Approximation bounds for estimated transition costs with decision-preserving guarantees (§15).
6. A corrected Bellman recursion separating control action from state realization (§16).
7. Polynomial-time and implicit-search algorithms with LSAP-based admissible heuristics (§17–§18).
8. Complexity analysis with PSPACE-hardness results grounded in Mundhenk et al. (2000) and Hearn & Demaine (§19).
9. A constrained formulation for safety-critical systems with peak-constraint edge pruning (§20).
10. A PAC-learning bound for decision-preserving transition-cost estimation (§21).
11. An explicit non-claim of novelty and a research program (§22–§23).

