5. Task State and Task Utility

Definition 5.1 (Task state). A task state is  T = (\tau, b, \delta)  where  \tau  is the objective,  b  is a resource budget, and  \delta  is an information/uncertainty state.

Definition 5.2 (Task dynamics). The task evolves according to a Markov process  T_{t+1} \sim P_T(\cdot \mid T_t) .

Definition 5.3 (Task utility). Let  Q: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{T} \to \mathbb{R}  assign expected task utility. A typical decomposition:

Q(G,x,T) = w_1 A + w_2 R + w_3 S + w_4 C - w_5 L - w_6 F

where  A  = accuracy,  R  = reliability,  S  = safety,  C  = coverage,  L  = latency,  F  = failure probability. The decomposition is application-dependent.

Assumption 5.4 (Boundedness). There exist  Q_{\min}, Q_{\max} \in \mathbb{R}  such that  Q_{\min} \le Q(s,T) \le Q_{\max}  for all  (s,T) .

---

6. Structural Distance Is Not Transition Cost

Motivating Example 6.1. Construct  G  with a high-context language model  M_1  feeding a critical verifier  M_2 . Let  G_A  remove five inexpensive peripheral edges. Let  G_B  remove the single edge  (M_1, M_2) . Under any structural measure counting edits, adjust edge multiplicities so the two distances coincide:  d_{\text{struct}}(G, G_A) = d_{\text{struct}}(G, G_B) = 5 . Operationally, removing  (M_1, M_2)  destroys the verifier's context and triggers re-initialization costing, say, 0.3 utility units, while removing the five peripheral edges costs 0.02 utility units total. Hence  C_T(s, s_A) = 0.02 \ll C_T(s, s_B) = 0.30  even though  d_{\text{struct}}  is equal.

Observation 6.2. Structural distance is not a sufficient statistic for reconfiguration decisions.

