7. Feasible Reconfiguration Space

Definition 7.1 (Feasibility). Let  h_j(s,T) \le 0  for  j = 1, \dots, m  be transition constraints (memory, latency, reliability, safety, bandwidth). Define

\mathcal{S}_{\text{feas}}(T) = \{s \in \mathcal{S} : h_j(s,T) \le 0 \ \forall j\}.

Definition 7.2 (Admissible transition). Define the admissible relation  A_T(s,s') \in \{0,1\} . We write  s \to_T s'  when  A_T(s,s') = 1 .

Definition 7.3 (Reconfiguration graph). The reconfiguration graph in the augmented state space is

\mathfrak{R}_T = (\mathcal{S}_{\text{feas}}(T), \mathcal{E}_T)

where  (s,s') \in \mathcal{E}_T  iff  s \to_T s' .

Proposition 7.4 (Endpoint feasibility does not imply transition feasibility). There exist  s, s' \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{feas}}(T)  with no admissible path from  s  to  s'  in  \mathfrak{R}_T .

Proof. Standard in reconfiguration theory. NCL-reachability is PSPACE-complete even for planar constraint graphs. Independent-set reconfiguration and shortest-path reconfiguration also exhibit endpoint-feasible but transition-infeasible pairs.  \square 

---

8. Elementary Transition Cost

Definition 8.1 (Elementary transition cost). Let  c_T: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{S} \to [0, \infty]  assign a nonnegative cost to an admissible elementary transition.

Assumption 8.2 (Nonnegativity).  c_T(s,s') \ge 0  for all admissible transitions.

Assumption 8.3 (Admissibility consistency).  c_T(s,s') = +\infty  iff  A_T(s,s') = 0 .

Definition 8.4 (Decomposition template).

c_T = c_{\text{edit}} + c_{\text{context}} + c_{\text{comm}} + c_{\text{latency}} + c_{\text{sync}} + c_{\text{risk}} + c_{\text{resource}}.

This is not required to be additive in every implementation.

Remark 8.5 (Double-counting warning). Context loss may be modeled either as a transition cost  c_{\text{context}}  or as a reduction in future  Q . Using both without explicit accounting double-counts the same phenomenon.

