9. Task-Aware Reconfiguration Cost

Definition 9.1 (Reconfiguration trajectory). A trajectory from  s  to  s'  is a sequence  \Gamma = (s_0, s_1, \dots, s_K)  with  s_0 = s ,  s_K = s' , and  (s_k, s_{k+1}) \in \mathcal{E}_T  for all  k .

Definition 9.2 (Admissible trajectory set). Let  \mathcal{A}_T(s,s') = \{\Gamma : s \leadsto s' \text{ feasible, all intermediate constraints satisfied}\} .

Definition 9.3 (Task-aware reconfiguration cost).

\boxed{ C_T(s,s') = \inf_{\Gamma \in \mathcal{A}_T(s,s')} \sum_{k=0}^{K-1} c_T(s_k, s_{k+1}) }

subject to  s_0 = s ,  s_K = s' ,  x_{k+1} = F_T(x_k, G_k, G_{k+1}) , and  h_j(s_k, T) \le 0  for all  k, j .

Convention 9.4. If  \mathcal{A}_T(s,s') = \emptyset , set  C_T(s,s') = +\infty . Define  C_T(s,s) = 0 .

Lemma 9.5 (Well-definedness — finite state space). If  \mathcal{S}  is finite and  c_T \ge 0 , then the infimum in Definition 9.3 is attained.

Proof. The set  \mathcal{A}_T(s,s')  is a subset of finite-length sequences over a finite alphabet. Any non-simple trajectory contains a cycle, which can be removed without increasing cost because  c_T \ge 0 . Hence the infimum is attained on a simple trajectory.  \square 

Lemma 9.6 (Well-definedness — continuous state space). If  \mathcal{G}_0  is finite,  \mathcal{X}  is a compact metric space,  F_T(x, G, G')  is continuous in  x , and  c_T(s,s')  is lower semi-continuous on  \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{S} , then for any feasible pair  (s,s') ,  C_T(s,s')  attains its minimum.

Proof. The set of admissible trajectories of length  K \le |\mathcal{G}_0|  is a finite union of compact sets (products of compact  \mathcal{X}  along each topology sequence). The cost functional is lower semi-continuous on each compact set, so the infimum is attained by the extreme value theorem.  \square 

