10. Directed Triangle Inequality

Theorem 10.1 (Directed triangle inequality — memoryless admissibility). Assume:

1.  c_T(s,s') \ge 0 ;
2. admissibility is memoryless:  A_T(s,s')  depends only on  (s,s') , not on trajectory history;
3. the state-transition dynamics  F_T  (or  P_T ) satisfy the Markov property: the terminal state of any trajectory from  s_0  to  s_1  is a valid initial state for any trajectory from  s_1  to  s_2 .

Then

C_T(s_0, s_2) \le C_T(s_0, s_1) + C_T(s_1, s_2).

Proof. Let  \epsilon > 0 . Choose  \Gamma_1 \in \mathcal{A}_T(s_0, s_1)  with  J(\Gamma_1) \le C_T(s_0, s_1) + \epsilon , and  \Gamma_2 \in \mathcal{A}_T(s_1, s_2)  with  J(\Gamma_2) \le C_T(s_1, s_2) + \epsilon . Concatenate:  \Gamma = \Gamma_1 \circ \Gamma_2 \in \mathcal{A}_T(s_0, s_2) . Then

C_T(s_0, s_2) \le J(\Gamma) = J(\Gamma_1) + J(\Gamma_2) \le C_T(s_0, s_1) + C_T(s_1, s_2) + 2\epsilon.

Letting  \epsilon \to 0  gives the result.  \square 

Remark 10.2 (History-dependent admissibility). If admissibility depends on trajectory history (e.g., a budget consumed along the path), the concatenation property may fail. In that case, the triangle inequality takes the relaxed form:

C_T(s_0, s_2) \le C_T(s_0, s_1) + C_T(s_1, s_2) + \inf_{x_1 \in \mathcal{X}} c_{\text{reset}}(x_1^{\text{term}}, x_1^{\text{init}})

where  c_{\text{reset}}  is the cost of matching the terminal state of  \Gamma_1  to the initial state of  \Gamma_2 .

Corollary 10.3 (Quasi-pseudometric structure). Under the hypotheses of Theorem 10.1,  C_T(\cdot, \cdot)  is a directed quasi-pseudometric on  \mathcal{S} .

Remark 10.4 (Asymmetry). In general,  C_T(s,s') \ne C_T(s',s) . A target model may be initialized from cached state cheaply while returning to the source requires expensive reconstruction.

Remark 10.5 (Identity).  C_T(s,s') = 0  does not imply  s = s' . Two distinct configurations may have zero-cost transitions if they are operationally equivalent under  T . Hence  C_T  is a pseudometric, not a metric.

