11. Static Switching Theorem

Definition 11.1 (Net reconfiguration value).

\Delta_T(s,s') = Q(s',T) - Q(s,T) - \lambda C_T(s,s').

Theorem 11.2 (Static switching threshold). Assume:

1.  Q  and  C_T  are as above;
2.  \lambda > 0 ;
3. no future consequences beyond the current decision (myopic setting).

Then switching from  s  to  s'  is optimal iff

\boxed{ Q(s',T) - Q(s,T) > \lambda C_T(s,s'). }

Proof. The utility of staying is  U_{\text{stay}} = Q(s,T) . The utility of switching is  U_{\text{switch}} = Q(s',T) - \lambda C_T(s,s') . Switching is strictly preferred iff  U_{\text{switch}} > U_{\text{stay}} , which rearranges to the stated condition.  \square 

Corollary 11.3 (Architectural inertia). If  0 < Q(s',T) - Q(s,T) \le \lambda C_T(s,s') , the current topology is retained even though  s'  has strictly higher raw task quality.

---

12. Hysteresis Band Characterization

Setup. Consider two configurations  s_A, s_B  and define

\Delta Q(T) = Q(s_B,T) - Q(s_A,T).

Let  C_{AB} = C_T(s_A,s_B)  and  C_{BA} = C_T(s_B,s_A) .

Theorem 12.1 (Hysteresis band). Under the static threshold:

·  A \to B  is optimal iff  \Delta Q > \lambda C_{AB} .
·  B \to A  is optimal iff  \Delta Q < -\lambda C_{BA} .

Consequently, the persistence region is

\boxed{ -\lambda C_{BA} \le \Delta Q \le \lambda C_{AB}. }

Proof. Direct from Theorem 11.2 applied to each direction.  \square 

Corollary 12.2 (Band width). The band width is  W = \lambda (C_{AB} + C_{BA}) . If  C_{AB} = C_{BA} = C , then  W = 2\lambda C .

Corollary 12.3 (Chattering suppression). Higher transition costs widen the persistence region and suppress architectural chattering.

Proposition 12.4 (Dynamic hysteresis band). In the infinite-horizon stationary setting with discount factor  \gamma \in (0,1) , let  V(s,T)  be the optimal value function. The system at state  s_A  remains in  s_A  rather than transitioning to  s_B  if and only if:

Q(s_B, T) - Q(s_A, T) \le \lambda C_T(s_A, s_B) + \gamma \left( \mathbb{E}[V(s_A, T')] - \mathbb{E}[V(s_B, T')] \right).

The dynamic persistence region is therefore shifted by the continuation gap  \Delta V_{\text{cont}} = \gamma (\mathbb{E}[V(s_A)] - \mathbb{E}[V(s_B)]) .

Proof. From the Bellman optimality equation (Theorem 15.1), staying in  s_A  is optimal iff

Q(s_A,T) + \gamma \mathbb{E}[V(s_A,T')] \ge Q(s_B,T) - \lambda C_T(s_A,s_B) + \gamma \mathbb{E}[V(s_B,T')].

Rearranging gives the stated condition.  \square 

