14. Dynamic Programming Formulation

Definition 14.1 (Sequential reconfiguration problem).

\max_{\{G_t\}} \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{t=0}^{H} \gamma^t Q(s_t,T_t) - \lambda \sum_{t=0}^{H-1} \gamma^t C_{T_t}(s_t,s_{t+1}) \right]

subject to  s_t \in \mathcal{S}_{\text{feas}}(T_t) ,  (s_t, s_{t+1}) \in \mathcal{E}_{T_t} ,  x_{t+1} = F_{T_t}(x_t, G_t, G_{t+1}) .

Remark 14.2 (Action–state separation). The optimization variable is the topology sequence  \{G_t\} , not the augmented configurations  \{s_t\} . The state component  x_{t+1}  is determined by the dynamics. This distinction is essential.

Theorem 14.3 (Bellman recursion). Let  V_t(s,T)  denote the optimal expected future value from time  t  with configuration  s = (G,x)  and task  T . Then

\boxed{ V_t(G,x,T) = \max_{G' \in \mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}}(G,x,T)} \mathbb{E}_{X' \sim P_T(\cdot \mid x, G, G')} \left[ Q(G', X', T) - \lambda c_T((G,x), (G', X')) + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{T' \mid T} V_{t+1}(G', X', T') \right] }

with  \mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}}(G,x,T) = \{G' \in \mathcal{G}_0 : (G,x) \to_T (G', x') \text{ for some } x' \in \mathcal{X}\} \cup \{G\} , and boundary condition  V_H(s,T) = Q(s,T) .

Proof. This is the standard Bellman optimality equation for a finite-horizon MDP whose state is  (s,T) , action is  G' , stage reward is  Q(G', X', T) - \lambda c_T((G,x), (G', X')) , and transition kernel is induced by  (T', P_T) . The "stay" action  G' = G  is included with  c_T(s,s) = 0 . Existence of the maximum follows from finiteness of  \mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}} .  \square 

Corollary 14.4 (Option value). The optimal  G_t  need not maximize  Q(\cdot, T_t) . A configuration with slightly lower immediate utility may be chosen if it has lower future transition costs or better future accessibility.

Corollary 14.5 (Infinite-horizon). If  \gamma < 1 ,  Q  is bounded, and admissible transition costs are bounded, the infinite-horizon value function  V(s,T)  satisfies

V(s,T) = \max_{G'} \mathbb{E}_{X'} \left[ Q(G',X',T) - \lambda c_T(s,(G',X')) + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{T'} V(G',X',T') \right]

and is the unique fixed point of the Bellman operator in the sup-norm.

Proof. The Bellman operator is a  \gamma -contraction on the Banach space of bounded functions under sup-norm. Banach fixed-point theorem applies.  \square 

