16. Complexity Analysis

Theorem 16.1 (Explicit-space complexity). If  \mathcal{S}  is finite and explicitly represented, computing  C_T(s,s')  is polynomial in  |\mathcal{S}| .

Proof. Dijkstra's algorithm on the reconfiguration graph  \mathfrak{R}_T  runs in  O(|\mathcal{E}_T| + |\mathcal{S}_{\text{feas}}| \log |\mathcal{S}_{\text{feas}}|) .  \square 

Theorem 16.2 (Implicit-space hardness). If  \mathcal{S}  is represented implicitly (e.g., as all subgraphs of a host graph, all linear extensions, all feasible colorings), computing  C_T(s,s')  is PSPACE-hard in general.

Proof sketch. Reduction from Nondeterministic Constraint Logic (NCL) reachability, which is PSPACE-complete even for planar graphs with restricted vertex configurations. NCL configurations map to topologies, valid edge reversals map to elementary transitions, and the cost structure is preserved by setting  c_T = 1  for all admissible transitions. The cost bound  K  encodes the path length. Hence RECONFIG-COST is PSPACE-hard.  \square 

Theorem 16.3 (Dynamic optimization hardness). The Bellman recursion over  \mathcal{S}  is PSPACE-hard when  \mathcal{S}  is implicit and the horizon is finite.

Proof. The Bellman recursion involves maximization over  \mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}}(s,T) , whose size is exponential in the host graph. This is the action space of a succinctly represented MDP. Policy existence for finite-horizon succinctly represented MDPs is PSPACE-complete (Mundhenk et al., 2000).  \square 

Remark 16.4 (Implicit graph search). The polynomial bound in Theorem 16.1 refers to the explicitly enumerated configuration space. The original topology problem is compactly represented, and the explicit configuration space may be exponentially large. The distinction is:

\text{polynomial in } |\mathcal{S}| \neq \text{polynomial in compact input size}.

Remark 16.5 (Practical implications). Practical implementations must use candidate generation, bounded-horizon rollout, admissible heuristics (LSAP), approximate transition costs with error bounds, learned proposals, and integer programming for restricted spaces.

---

17. Constrained Formulation for Safety-Critical Systems

For safety-critical systems, scalarization via  \lambda  is inappropriate. Use:

\max_{\{G_t\}} \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_t \gamma^t Q(s_t,T_t) \right]

subject to

C_{T_t}(s_t, s_{t+1}) \le C_{\max}, \quad P_{\text{fail}}(s_t, s_{t+1}) \le p_{\max}, \quad L(s_t, s_{t+1}) \le L_{\max}.

Remark 17.1 (Peak vs. expected constraints). For peak (per-step) constraints, edge pruning is exact. For expected-cost constraints, Lagrangian relaxation with projected subgradient is appropriate under convexity and Slater's condition. Peak-cost constrained MDPs may exhibit a duality gap.

Remark 17.2 (Safe reconfiguration hierarchy).

\text{Safety feasibility} \rightarrow \text{Reachability} \rightarrow \text{Resource feasibility} \rightarrow \text{Utility optimization}.

Optimization occurs only over  \mathcal{A}_{\text{safe}}(s,T) \subseteq \mathcal{A}_T(s) .

