18. Learning Transition Costs

Definition 18.1 (Learned transition cost). Parameterize  \widehat{C}_\theta(s,s',T) . Given observed transitions  (s_i, s_i', T_i, c_i) , minimize

\mathcal{L}(\theta) = \sum_i \left[ \widehat{C}_\theta(s_i, s_i', T_i) - c_i \right]^2.

Theorem 18.2 (PAC decision-preserving sample complexity). Let  \mathcal{F} = \{ \widehat{C}_\theta : \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{T} \to [0, C_{\max}] \}  be a bounded hypothesis class with pseudo-dimension  d_{\mathcal{F}} . For any  \epsilon, \delta > 0 , if the sample size  N  satisfies

N \ge \mathcal{O}\left( \frac{C_{\max}^2}{\epsilon^2} \left[ d_{\mathcal{F}} \log\left(\frac{C_{\max}}{\epsilon}\right) + \log\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right) \right] \right),

then with probability at least  1 - \delta , the empirical estimator achieves uniform estimation error  \|\widehat{C}_\theta - C_T\|_\infty \le \epsilon . Furthermore, for any pair  (s,s')  with decision margin  M_T(s,s') = |\Delta Q_T(s,s') - \lambda C_T(s,s')| > \lambda \epsilon , the decision policy induced by  \widehat{C}_\theta  matches the exact optimal decision under  C_T .

Proof sketch. Standard uniform convergence via pseudo-dimension (Pollard's theorem). The decision-preservation follows from Theorem 13.2: if the estimation error is at most  \epsilon , the decision can only change inside a margin of  \lambda \epsilon .  \square 

Remark 18.3 (Online learning with switching costs). The minimax regret for adversarial bandits with switching costs is  \widetilde{\Theta}(T^{2/3})  (Dekel et al., 2014). This provides a theoretical anchor for regret bounds in the online reconfiguration setting.

Corollary 18.4 (Regret bound for online reconfiguration). Under adversarial task sequences with switching costs, the minimax regret of any online reconfiguration policy is at least  \Omega(T^{2/3})  and achievable up to logarithmic factors.

