19. Novelty Position and Non-Claims

The strongest claim supportable at this stage:

Existing mathematics provides separate tools for graph edit distance, graph reconfiguration, switching costs, dynamic programming, and network comparison. This work investigates a unified task-aware formulation in which computational topology is a controllable state variable and the decision to change topology depends explicitly on the operational cost of reaching the new topology.

Not claimed:

· graph reconfiguration theory is new;
· switching costs are new;
· hysteresis is new;
· graph edit distance is new;
· dynamic model routing is new;
· multi-agent dynamic topology is new;
· dynamic programming over discrete architectures is new;
·  C_T  is proven to be a metric;
· the framework has been experimentally validated.

To be established by future work:

· whether the unified formulation yields genuinely new theorems;
· whether the empirical predictions hold;
· whether the complexity barriers can be circumvented in practice.

