Phase I — Mathematical foundations. Formalize  \mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}_T, C_T, Q .

Phase II — Transition geometry. Study  C_T  as a directed quasi-pseudometric; characterize connected components of  \mathfrak{R}_T .

Phase III — Switching theorem. Prove static and dynamic threshold results; extend to stochastic  Q  and  C_T .

Phase IV — Hysteresis. Derive persistence regions; characterize dependence on task variation and cost asymmetry.

Phase V — Dynamic programming. Establish existence, uniqueness, and contraction properties; develop approximate DP with error bounds.

Phase VI — Uncertainty. Robust switching; distributionally robust formulations.

Phase VII — Approximation. Develop tractable estimators with provable bounds; study sample complexity.

Phase VIII — Simulation. Synthetic heterogeneous AI systems.

Phase IX — Empirical AI experiment. Compare fixed, endpoint-optimal, greedy transition-aware, and dynamic policies.

Phase X — Novelty audit. Only after mathematical and empirical results are established should a strong novelty claim be made.

Open Problems

1. Complexity classification. For which classes of computational topologies (trees, bounded-treewidth, planar) is  C_T  polynomial-time computable? Reconfiguration is FPT parameterized by treewidth for MSO-definable problems, but PSPACE-complete for treewidth at most  \ell  for some vertex-subset problems.
2. Approximation algorithms. Does there exist a PTAS for  C_T  under metric edit costs? GED has no PTAS unless P = NP, but restricted topology classes may admit approximation schemes.
3. Learning transition costs. What is the sample complexity of learning  C_T  to within  \epsilon ? The PAC bound in Theorem 18.2 provides an upper bound depending on pseudo-dimension.
4. Hysteresis under stochastic tasks. How does the persistence region scale with task variance?
5. Option value. Can the Bellman recursion be decomposed into immediate utility plus an explicit option-value term?
6. Constrained MDP. Does the constrained formulation admit a polynomial-time algorithm under convexity assumptions? Peak-cost constraints may have duality gaps.
7. Emergent topology. Can reconfiguration policies be learned end-to-end with differentiable relaxations?

