21. Conclusion

We have developed a mathematically rigorous framework for task-aware transition costs between computational topologies. The central object

C_T(s,s') = \inf_{\Gamma \in \mathcal{A}_T(s,s')} \sum_{k=0}^{K-1} c_T(s_k, s_{k+1})

is a directed shortest-path cost satisfying a triangle inequality under memoryless admissibility. The static switching rule  Q(s',T) - Q(s,T) > \lambda C_T(s,s')  is optimal in the myopic setting. The hysteresis band  -\lambda C_{BA} \le \Delta Q \le \lambda C_{AB}  characterizes architectural persistence. Approximation bounds, the corrected Bellman recursion with action–state separation, polynomial-time algorithms for explicit spaces, LSAP-based admissible heuristics for implicit spaces, and PSPACE-hardness results grounded in Mundhenk et al. (2000) and Hearn & Demaine have been established.

Whether the unified formulation yields genuinely new mathematics remains an open question. The framework is offered as a research program, not a finished theory.

