Appendix A. Foundational Definitions and Notation

A.1 Set-Theoretic Conventions

We adopt the following conventions throughout.

·  \mathbb{N} = \{0, 1, 2, \dots\} ,  \mathbb{N}^+ = \{1, 2, \dots\} .
·  \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} = [0, \infty) ,  \overline{\mathbb{R}}_{\ge 0} = [0, \infty] .
· For a set  X ,  |X|  denotes cardinality,  2^X  the power set.
· For a metric space  (X, d) ,  \mathcal{B}(X)  denotes the Borel  \sigma -algebra.
·  \mathbf{1}[\cdot]  denotes the indicator function.

A.2 Graph-Theoretic Conventions

Definition A.1 (Labeled directed graph). A labeled directed graph is a tuple  G = (V, E, \xi, \omega)  where:

·  V  is a finite vertex set;
·  E \subseteq V \times V \setminus \{(v,v) : v \in V\}  is a set of directed edges (no self-loops by convention);
·  \xi: V \to \mathcal{X}_V  is a vertex labeling function into a label set  \mathcal{X}_V ;
·  \omega: E \to \mathcal{X}_E  is an edge labeling function into a label set  \mathcal{X}_E .

Definition A.2 (Computational topology). A computational topology is a tuple  G = (V, E, \xi, \omega, \rho)  where  (V, E, \xi, \omega)  is a labeled directed graph and  \rho  is a routing/orchestration semantics: an assignment to each vertex  v \in V  of an execution function

\rho(v): \prod_{u \in N^-(v)} \mathcal{Y}_u \to \mathcal{Y}_v

where  N^-(v) = \{u \in V : (u,v) \in E\}  is the in-neighborhood,  \mathcal{Y}_u  is the output space of vertex  u , and  \mathcal{Y}_v  is the output space of vertex  v .

Definition A.3 (Topology space). Let  \mathcal{G}_0  denote the ambient space of all computational topologies compatible with a fixed system specification  \Sigma . Concretely,  \mathcal{G}_0  is the set of all  G = (V, E, \xi, \omega, \rho)  such that:

·  V \subseteq V_{\max}  for a fixed maximum vertex set  V_{\max} ;
·  E \subseteq V \times V ;
·  \xi(v) \in \mathcal{X}_V  for all  v \in V ;
·  \omega(e) \in \mathcal{X}_E  for all  e \in E ;
·  \rho  is well-defined for every vertex.

Remark A.4. If  V_{\max}  is fixed and  n = |V_{\max}|^2 - |V_{\max}|  is the number of possible directed edges, then  |\mathcal{G}_0| \le 3^n \cdot |\mathcal{X}_V|^{|V_{\max}|} \cdot |\mathcal{X}_E|^n  where  3^n  counts the edge subsets. Hence  |\mathcal{G}_0|  is finite but exponential in  |V_{\max}| .

A.3 State-Space Conventions

Definition A.5 (Computational state space). The computational state space  \mathcal{X}  is a measurable space  (\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{X})) . We consider two cases:

· Discrete case:  \mathcal{X}  is a finite or countable set with the discrete  \sigma -algebra.
· Continuous case:  \mathcal{X} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d  is a compact metric space with the Borel  \sigma -algebra.

Definition A.6 (Augmented configuration space). The augmented configuration space is

\mathcal{S} = \mathcal{G}_0 \times \mathcal{X}

with the product  \sigma -algebra  \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{S}) = 2^{\mathcal{G}_0} \otimes \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{X}) .

Definition A.7 (Feasibility constraints). Let  h_j: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{T} \to \mathbb{R}  for  j = 1, \dots, m . The feasible set under task  T  is

\mathcal{S}_{\text{feas}}(T) = \{ s \in \mathcal{S} : h_j(s,T) \le 0 \ \forall j \}.

A.4 Task and Dynamics Conventions

Definition A.8 (Task space). The task space  \mathcal{T}  is a measurable space  (\mathcal{T}, \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{T})) . A task state  T \in \mathcal{T}  is  T = (\tau, b, \delta)  with  \tau  the objective,  b  the resource budget, and  \delta  the uncertainty state.

Definition A.9 (Deterministic state dynamics). A deterministic state-transition function is a map

F_T: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{G}_0 \times \mathcal{G}_0 \to \mathcal{X}, \qquad x' = F_T(x, G, G').

Definition A.10 (Stochastic state dynamics). A stochastic state-transition kernel is a map

P_T: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{G}_0 \times \mathcal{G}_0 \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})

where  \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{X})  is the space of probability measures on  (\mathcal{X}, \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{X})) . We write  x' \sim P_T(\cdot \mid x, G, G') .

Definition A.11 (Task dynamics). The task evolves according to a Markov kernel

P_T^{\text{task}}: \mathcal{T} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathcal{T}), \qquad T' \sim P_T^{\text{task}}(\cdot \mid T).

