Appendix B. Full Proofs

B.1 Well-Definedness of the Infimum

Lemma B.1 (Finite state space). Let  \mathcal{S}  be finite and  c_T \ge 0 . Then for every pair  (s, s') \in \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{S} , the infimum

C_T(s,s') = \inf_{\Gamma \in \mathcal{A}_T(s,s')} J(\Gamma)

is attained.

Proof. Since  \mathcal{S}  is finite, the set  \mathcal{A}_T(s,s')  is a subset of  \bigcup_{K=0}^{\infty} \mathcal{S}^{K+1} . For any trajectory  \Gamma = (s_0, \dots, s_K) , if  K > |\mathcal{S}| - 1 , then  \Gamma  contains a repeated state  s_i = s_j  with  i < j . Removing the cycle  (s_i, \dots, s_{j-1})  yields a shorter trajectory  \Gamma'  with  J(\Gamma') \le J(\Gamma)  because  c_T \ge 0 . Iterating, every trajectory can be reduced to a simple trajectory (no repeated states) without increasing cost. Hence it suffices to consider trajectories of length  K \le |\mathcal{S}| - 1 , of which there are finitely many. The infimum over a finite set is attained.  \square 

Lemma B.2 (Continuous state space). Suppose  \mathcal{G}_0  is finite,  \mathcal{X}  is compact metric,  F_T(x, G, G')  is continuous in  x  for each  (G, G') , and  c_T  is lower semi-continuous on  \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{S} . Then for every feasible pair  (s, s') ,  C_T(s,s')  is attained.

Proof. Fix a topology sequence  \mathbf{G} = (G_0, \dots, G_K)  with  G_0 = G ,  G_K = G' . Consider the set  \mathcal{X}^{K+1}  of state sequences. The feasibility conditions define a closed subset

\mathcal{F}_{\mathbf{G}} = \{ (x_0, \dots, x_K) \in \mathcal{X}^{K+1} : x_{k+1} = F_T(x_k, G_k, G_{k+1}), \ h_j(G_k, x_k, T) \le 0 \ \forall k, j \}.

Since  \mathcal{X}  is compact and  F_T, h_j  are continuous,  \mathcal{F}_{\mathbf{G}}  is closed in  \mathcal{X}^{K+1}  and hence compact. The cost functional

J_{\mathbf{G}}(x_0, \dots, x_K) = \sum_{k=0}^{K-1} c_T((G_k, x_k), (G_{k+1}, x_{k+1}))

is lower semi-continuous on  \mathcal{F}_{\mathbf{G}} . By the extreme value theorem for lower semi-continuous functions on compact sets,  J_{\mathbf{G}}  attains its minimum on  \mathcal{F}_{\mathbf{G}} .

Since  \mathcal{G}_0  is finite, there are finitely many topology sequences of length  K \le |\mathcal{G}_0| - 1 . The global infimum is the minimum over finitely many attained minima, hence attained.  \square 

Corollary B.3. Under the hypotheses of Lemma B.1 or B.2, there exists an optimal trajectory  \Gamma^* \in \mathcal{A}_T(s,s')  with  J(\Gamma^*) = C_T(s,s') .

B.2 Directed Triangle Inequality — Full Proof

Theorem B.4 (Directed triangle inequality — memoryless admissibility). Let  c_T \ge 0 . Suppose admissibility is memoryless, i.e.,  A_T(s,s')  depends only on the ordered pair  (s,s') , and the state dynamics satisfy the Markov property: for any trajectory  \Gamma_1: s_0 \leadsto s_1  and any trajectory  \Gamma_2: s_1 \leadsto s_2 , the concatenation  \Gamma_1 \circ \Gamma_2  is admissible from  s_0  to  s_2 . Then

C_T(s_0, s_2) \le C_T(s_0, s_1) + C_T(s_1, s_2) \qquad \forall s_0, s_1, s_2 \in \mathcal{S}.

Proof. Fix  \epsilon > 0 .

Step 1: Existence of  \epsilon -optimal trajectories. By definition of infimum, there exist  \Gamma_1 \in \mathcal{A}_T(s_0, s_1)  and  \Gamma_2 \in \mathcal{A}_T(s_1, s_2)  such that

J(\Gamma_1) \le C_T(s_0, s_1) + \epsilon, \qquad J(\Gamma_2) \le C_T(s_1, s_2) + \epsilon.

Step 2: Concatenation. Define  \Gamma = \Gamma_1 \circ \Gamma_2 . By the Markov property,  \Gamma \in \mathcal{A}_T(s_0, s_2) .

Step 3: Cost additivity. By definition of the cost functional,

J(\Gamma) = J(\Gamma_1) + J(\Gamma_2).

Step 4: Upper bound.

C_T(s_0, s_2) \le J(\Gamma) = J(\Gamma_1) + J(\Gamma_2) \le C_T(s_0, s_1) + C_T(s_1, s_2) + 2\epsilon.

Step 5: Limit. Since  \epsilon > 0  is arbitrary, let  \epsilon \to 0 :

C_T(s_0, s_2) \le C_T(s_0, s_1) + C_T(s_1, s_2).

 \square 

Remark B.5 (History-dependent admissibility). If admissibility depends on trajectory history via a consumable resource (e.g., a total energy budget), the concatenation property may fail. In that case, the correct statement is: for any  \Gamma_1: s_0 \leadsto s_1  and  \Gamma_2: s_1 \leadsto s_2 ,

C_T(s_0, s_2) \le J(\Gamma_1) + J(\Gamma_2) + c_{\text{reset}}(x_1^{\text{term}}, x_1^{\text{init}})

where the reset cost accounts for reconciling terminal and initial states.

B.3 Directed Quasi-Pseudometric Properties

Proposition B.6. Under the hypotheses of Theorem B.4:

1.  C_T(s,s) = 0  for all  s \in \mathcal{S} .
2.  C_T(s_0, s_2) \le C_T(s_0, s_1) + C_T(s_1, s_2)  (directed triangle inequality).
3.  C_T(s,s') \ge 0  (nonnegativity).

Proof. (1) The trivial trajectory  \Gamma = (s)  has cost zero, so  C_T(s,s) \le 0 . Since  c_T \ge 0 ,  C_T(s,s) \ge 0 . (2) Theorem B.4. (3) Since each  c_T \ge 0 , any finite sum is nonnegative; the infimum of nonnegative quantities is nonnegative.  \square 

Remark B.7 (Failure of symmetry).  C_T(s,s') = C_T(s',s)  is not guaranteed. The proof of Theorem B.4 makes no use of symmetry. A concrete asymmetric example appears in Appendix G.1.

Remark B.8 (Failure of identity of indiscernibles).  C_T(s,s') = 0  does not imply  s = s' . If two distinct configurations  s \ne s'  are operationally equivalent under  T , then  c_T(s,s') = 0  and  c_T(s',s) = 0 , so  C_T(s,s') = C_T(s',s) = 0 . A concrete example appears in Appendix G.2.

B.4 Static Switching Theorem — Full Proof

Theorem B.9 (Static switching). Under the myopic setting with  \lambda > 0 , switching from  s  to  s'  is optimal iff

Q(s',T) - Q(s,T) > \lambda C_T(s,s').

Proof.

Necessity. Suppose switching is optimal. Then

Q(s',T) - \lambda C_T(s,s') \ge Q(s,T).

Rearranging:

Q(s',T) - Q(s,T) \ge \lambda C_T(s,s').

For strict optimality, we require  > . For weak optimality,  \ge .

Sufficiency. Suppose  Q(s',T) - Q(s,T) > \lambda C_T(s,s') . Then

Q(s',T) - \lambda C_T(s,s') > Q(s,T),

so switching yields strictly higher utility than staying.  \square 

Corollary B.10 (Hysteresis band). With  \Delta Q = Q(s_B, T) - Q(s_A, T) ,  C_{AB} = C_T(s_A, s_B) ,  C_{BA} = C_T(s_B, s_A) , the transitions  A \to B  and  B \to A  are both suboptimal iff

-\lambda C_{BA} \le \Delta Q \le \lambda C_{AB}.

Proof.  A \to B  is optimal iff  \Delta Q > \lambda C_{AB} .  B \to A  is optimal iff  Q(s_A,T) - Q(s_B,T) > \lambda C_{BA} , i.e.,  -\Delta Q > \lambda C_{BA} , i.e.,  \Delta Q < -\lambda C_{BA} . The complement of the union of these two conditions is  -\lambda C_{BA} \le \Delta Q \le \lambda C_{AB} .  \square 

B.5 Approximation Bound — Full Proof

Theorem B.11 (Decision error bound). Let  \widehat{C}_T  satisfy  |\widehat{C}_T(s,s') - C_T(s,s')| \le \epsilon  for all admissible pairs. Under the static threshold, the exact and approximate decision rules disagree only when

|\Delta Q_T(s,s') - \lambda C_T(s,s')| \le \lambda \epsilon.

Proof. Let  \Delta Q = Q(s',T) - Q(s,T) .

The exact rule switches iff  \Delta Q > \lambda C_T(s,s') .
The approximate rule switches iff  \Delta Q > \lambda \widehat{C}_T(s,s') .

Case 1:  \widehat{C}_T \ge C_T . Then  \lambda \widehat{C}_T \ge \lambda C_T . Disagreement occurs iff

\lambda C_T < \Delta Q \le \lambda \widehat{C}_T.

The width of this interval is  \lambda(\widehat{C}_T - C_T) \le \lambda \epsilon .

Case 2:  \widehat{C}_T < C_T . Then  \lambda \widehat{C}_T < \lambda C_T . Disagreement occurs iff

\lambda \widehat{C}_T < \Delta Q \le \lambda C_T.

The width is  \lambda(C_T - \widehat{C}_T) \le \lambda \epsilon .

In either case, disagreement occurs only when  \Delta Q  lies within  \lambda \epsilon  of  \lambda C_T .  \square 

B.6 Bellman Recursion — Existence and Uniqueness

Theorem B.12 (Finite-horizon Bellman). Consider the finite-horizon problem

\max_{\{G_t\}_{t=0}^{H}} \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_{t=0}^{H} \gamma^t Q(s_t, T_t) - \lambda \sum_{t=0}^{H-1} \gamma^t c_{T_t}(s_t, s_{t+1}) \right]

with  s_{t+1} = (G_{t+1}, X_{t+1}) ,  X_{t+1} \sim P_{T_t}(\cdot \mid x_t, G_t, G_{t+1}) . Then the value function

V_t(G, x, T) = \max_{G' \in \mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}}(G,x,T)} \mathbb{E}_{X'} \left[ Q(G', X', T) - \lambda c_T((G,x), (G', X')) + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{T'} V_{t+1}(G', X', T') \right]

is well-defined for all  t \in \{0, \dots, H\}  with terminal condition  V_H(G,x,T) = Q(G,x,T) .

Proof. By backward induction on  t .

Base case  t = H :  V_H = Q  is well-defined by Assumption 5.4 (boundedness).

Inductive step. Assume  V_{t+1}  is well-defined and bounded. The expression

\Psi(G', x, T) = \mathbb{E}_{X'} \left[ Q(G', X', T) - \lambda c_T((G,x), (G', X')) + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{T'} V_{t+1}(G', X', T') \right]

is bounded because:

·  Q  is bounded by Assumption 5.4;
·  c_T \ge 0  and admissible transitions have bounded cost by Assumption 8.2;
·  V_{t+1}  is bounded by the inductive hypothesis.

Hence  \Psi  is well-defined and finite. The maximum over the finite set  \mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}}(G,x,T)  exists.  \square 

Theorem B.13 (Infinite-horizon contraction). Let  \gamma \in (0,1) . Define the Bellman operator  \mathcal{B}  on bounded functions  V: \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{T} \to \mathbb{R}  by

(\mathcal{B}V)(s,T) = \max_{G' \in \mathcal{G}_{\text{adm}}(s,T)} \mathbb{E}_{X' \sim P_T} \left[ Q(G',X',T) - \lambda c_T(s, (G',X')) + \gamma \mathbb{E}_{T'} V((G',X'), T') \right].

Then  \mathcal{B}  is a  \gamma -contraction in the sup-norm  \|\cdot\|_\infty .

Proof. Let  V_1, V_2  be bounded functions. For any  (s,T) ,

|(\mathcal{B}V_1)(s,T) - (\mathcal{B}V_2)(s,T)| \le \max_{G'} \gamma |\mathbb{E}_{T'} [V_1((G',X'), T') - V_2((G',X'), T')]|.

By the tower property and boundedness,

|\mathbb{E}_{T'} [V_1 - V_2]| \le \|V_1 - V_2\|_\infty.

Hence

\|(\mathcal{B}V_1) - (\mathcal{B}V_2)\|_\infty \le \gamma \|V_1 - V_2\|_\infty.

Corollary B.14 (Unique fixed point). By the Banach fixed-point theorem,  \mathcal{B}  has a unique fixed point  V^* \in \mathcal{B}(\mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{T}) , and  V_n = \mathcal{B}^n V_0 \to V^*  in sup-norm for any bounded  V_0 .  \square 

B.7 PSPACE-Hardness of Implicit-Space Reconfiguration

Theorem B.15 (RECONFIG-COST is PSPACE-hard). The following decision problem is PSPACE-hard:

Instance: A host graph  \mathcal{H} , initial configuration  s = (G,x) , target configuration  s' = (G',x') , task  T , cost bound  K \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0} .

Question: Does there exist a trajectory  \Gamma \in \mathcal{A}_T(s,s')  with  J(\Gamma) \le K ?

Proof. We give a polynomial-time reduction from Nondeterministic Constraint Logic (NCL) reachability, which is PSPACE-complete even for planar graphs with restricted vertex configurations (Hearn & Demaine, 2005).

NCL specification. An NCL instance is a graph  \mathcal{N} = (V_{\mathcal{N}}, E_{\mathcal{N}})  with vertices of two types:

· AND vertices: three incident edges, one designated output, two designated inputs. Weight 2, minimum inflow 2.
· OR vertices: three incident edges. Weight 2, minimum inflow 2.

Each edge is in one of two orientations, and a move reverses an edge, maintaining the inflow constraint at every vertex. NCL-Reachability asks whether one given orientation can reach another via valid moves.

Reduction. Given an NCL instance  \mathcal{N}  with initial orientation  \sigma_0  and target orientation  \sigma^* :

1. Host graph. Set  \mathcal{H} = \mathcal{N}  with a global vertex  g^*  connected to all vertices of  \mathcal{N} . The global vertex is always active.
2. Topology encoding. Each edge  e \in E_{\mathcal{N}}  of  \mathcal{N}  corresponds to an edge  e \in E_{\mathcal{H}} . A topology  G  is defined by the subset of edges in  E_{\mathcal{H}}  that are active. An orientation  \sigma  of  \mathcal{N}  corresponds to a subset  E_\sigma \subseteq E_{\mathcal{H}}  with  e \in E_\sigma  iff  e  is oriented toward a specified endpoint.
3. Initial and target configurations. Set  G_0  = topology corresponding to  \sigma_0 ,  G^*  = topology corresponding to  \sigma^* . Set  x_0 = x^* = \emptyset  (empty state).
4. Elementary transitions. A valid NCL move reverses one edge  e , preserving the inflow constraint at both endpoints. In the topology encoding, this corresponds to changing the active status of  e , i.e.,  (G, G') \in \mathcal{E}_T  iff  G' = G \triangle \{e\}  for some  e \in E_{\mathcal{H}} .
5. Cost. Set  c_T(G, G') = 1  for all admissible transitions.
6. Cost bound. Set  K = |E_{\mathcal{H}}| .

Correctness. A valid NCL move sequence  \sigma_0 \to \sigma_1 \to \dots \to \sigma_k = \sigma^*  corresponds bijectively to a trajectory  G_0 \to G_1 \to \dots \to G_k = G^*  in the topology encoding. The number of moves equals the number of transitions equals the cost. Hence a bounded-cost trajectory exists iff a valid NCL move sequence exists. Since the reduction is polynomial-time computable and NCL-Reachability is PSPACE-complete, RECONFIG-COST is PSPACE-hard.  \square 

Theorem B.16 (Finite-horizon succinct MDP hardness). The Bellman recursion over an implicitly represented configuration space  \mathcal{S}  is PSPACE-hard for finite horizon  H .

Proof. The reduction of Theorem B.15 produces a finite-horizon MDP with horizon  H = K = |E_{\mathcal{H}}|  and a succinctly represented action space. By Mundhenk, Goldsmith, Lusena, and Allender (2000), the finite-horizon policy-existence problem for succinctly represented MDPs is PSPACE-complete. Hence the Bellman recursion is PSPACE-hard.  \square 

B.8 LSAP Admissibility

Theorem B.17 (LSAP lower bound). Let  \widehat{d}_{\text{LSAP}}(G,G')  denote the LSAP relaxation of the graph edit distance  d_{\text{edit}}(G,G') . Then

\widehat{d}_{\text{LSAP}}(G,G') \le d_{\text{edit}}(G,G')

for all  G, G' \in \mathcal{G}_0 .

Proof. The LSAP relaxation constructs a bipartite graph with vertex sets  V \cup \{\epsilon\}  (source vertices plus a dummy) and  V' \cup \{\epsilon\}  (target vertices plus a dummy). Each source vertex can be matched to at most one target vertex, with edge cost equal to the cost of editing the corresponding vertex (relabeling) or deleting/inserting. Edge edit costs are attached to vertex substitutions. The LSAP is solved in  O(|V|^3)  time via the Hungarian algorithm.

Any valid graph edit path induces a matching in the bipartite graph with cost equal to the edit path cost. Hence the minimum matching cost is at most the minimum edit path cost. Therefore  \widehat{d}_{\text{LSAP}} \le d_{\text{edit}} .  \square 

Corollary B.18 (Admissible heuristic). The function

h_{\text{LSAP}}(u) = c_{\min} \cdot \widehat{d}_{\text{LSAP}}(G_u, G_{s'})

is an admissible heuristic for A* search from  u  to  s' .

Proof. By Corollary 13.4 and Theorem B.17,

h_{\text{LSAP}}(u) = c_{\min} \widehat{d}_{\text{LSAP}}(G_u, G_{s'}) \le c_{\min} d_{\text{edit}}(G_u, G_{s'}) \le C_T(u, s').

 \square 

