Appendix C. Algorithm Analysis

C.1 Dijkstra Correctness

Theorem C.1. Algorithm 1 (Dijkstra) terminates with  \text{dist}[s'] = C_T(s,s')  for all reachable  s' .

Proof. Standard Dijkstra correctness for directed graphs with nonnegative edge weights. The relaxation step preserves the invariant that for every visited node  u ,  \text{dist}[u]  equals the length of the shortest  s -to- u  path.  \square 

Complexity. With a Fibonacci heap,  O(|\mathcal{E}_T| + |\mathcal{S}_{\text{feas}}| \log |\mathcal{S}_{\text{feas}}|) .

C.2 A* Correctness with LSAP Heuristic

Theorem C.2. Algorithm 2 (A*) with admissible heuristic  h_{\text{LSAP}}  returns the optimal cost  C_T(s,s') .

Proof. Standard A* optimality theorem (Hart, Nilsson, Raphael, 1968), requiring:

1. The graph is locally finite (each node has finitely many successors);
2. Edge costs are nonnegative;
3. The heuristic is admissible:  h(u) \le C_T(u, s')  for all  u .

Conditions (1) and (2) hold by Assumptions 8.2 and the finiteness of  \mathcal{G}_0 . Condition (3) holds by Corollary B.18.  \square 

Complexity. Worst case  O(|\mathcal{S}_{\text{feas}}| \log |\mathcal{S}_{\text{feas}}|) ; practical performance depends on heuristic quality.

C.3 Rollout Heuristic Analysis

Proposition C.3. Algorithm 3 (rollout) returns a policy  \pi^{\text{roll}}  satisfying

V^{\pi^{\text{roll}}}(s_0, T_0) \ge V^{\pi^{\text{base}}}(s_0, T_0)

where  \pi^{\text{base}}  is the base policy used to define the rollout.

Proof. Standard rollout theorem (Bertsekas, 2005). The rollout policy is at least as good as the base policy at every state, by construction of the rollout rollout step.  \square 

Remark C.4. The rollout heuristic does not in general equal the Bellman-optimal value. It is a one-step lookahead over a base policy.

C.4 Lagrangian Relaxation Convergence

Theorem C.5. Consider the constrained problem

\max_{\pi} \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_t \gamma^t Q(s_t, T_t) \right] \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_t \gamma^t c_T(s_t, s_{t+1}) \right] \le C_{\max}.

Assume Slater's condition: there exists  \pi_0  with expected cost strictly less than  C_{\max} . Then the Lagrangian dual has zero duality gap, and the projected subgradient iteration

\mu_{n+1} = \max(0, \mu_n + \eta_n \cdot (\mathbb{E}_{\pi_n}[\text{cost}] - C_{\max}))

converges to the optimal dual variable  \mu^*  for a suitable stepsize schedule  \eta_n  with  \sum_n \eta_n = \infty ,  \sum_n \eta_n^2 < \infty .

Proof. The Lagrangian  L(\pi, \mu) = \mathbb{E}[\text{utility}] - \mu(\mathbb{E}[\text{cost}] - C_{\max})  is concave in  \mu  (affine) and linear in the state-occupation measure of  \pi . Under Slater's condition, the strong duality theorem for convex programs applies. The projected subgradient iteration is the standard method for the dual problem; its convergence follows from Polyak's theorem under the stated stepsize conditions.  \square 

Remark C.6 (Peak constraints). For per-step (peak) constraints  c_T(s,s') \le C_{\max} , the Lagrangian approach does not apply directly because the constraint is not an expectation. Edge pruning (Algorithm 4) is exact.

