Appendix D. Worked Examples

D.1 Structural Distance vs. Transition Cost

Example D.1. Let  V_{\max} = \{M_1, M_2, M_3, V\}  with:

·  M_1  = language model;
·  M_2  = reasoning model;
·  M_3  = vision model;
·  V  = verifier.

Initial topology  G :

· Edges  E = \{(M_1, M_2), (M_2, V), (M_1, M_3), (M_3, V)\} .

Alternative  G_A : remove  (M_1, M_3)  and  (M_3, V) ; add  (M_1, V) . Net:  d_{\text{edit}}(G, G_A) = 3  (two deletions, one insertion).

Alternative  G_B : remove  (M_2, V) ; add  (M_1, V) . Net:  d_{\text{edit}}(G, G_B) = 2 .

Suppose the verifier  V  depends critically on  M_2 's reasoning output. Removing  (M_2, V)  requires re-initializing  V  and re-running the reasoning chain, costing 0.20 utility units. Removing  (M_1, M_3)  and  (M_3, V)  only affects the vision path, which is inactive for the current task, costing 0.01 utility units.

Then  C_T(G, G_A) = 0.01  even though  d_{\text{edit}}(G, G_A) = 3 , and  C_T(G, G_B) = 0.20  even though  d_{\text{edit}}(G, G_B) = 2 . This establishes that structural distance and operational cost are decoupled.

D.2 Hysteresis Band Numerical Example

Example D.2. Let  \lambda = 1 ,  C_{AB} = 0.05 ,  C_{BA} = 0.03 . Then:

·  A \to B  iff  \Delta Q > 0.05 ;
·  B \to A  iff  \Delta Q < -0.03 ;
· Persistence region:  -0.03 \le \Delta Q \le 0.05 .

If task distribution produces  \Delta Q \sim \mathcal{N}(0.01, 0.04^2) , then:

·  P(A \to B) = P(\Delta Q > 0.05) \approx 0.16 ;
·  P(B \to A) = P(\Delta Q < -0.03) \approx 0.16 ;
·  P(\text{stay}) \approx 0.68 .

The static analysis gives the switching probability under i.i.d. task sampling.

D.3 Dynamic Hysteresis Band

Example D.3. In the infinite-horizon setting, suppose:

·  Q(s_A, T) = 0.80 ;
·  Q(s_B, T) = 0.90 ;
·  \lambda = 1 ;
·  C_{AB} = 0.05 ,  C_{BA} = 0.05 ;
·  \gamma = 0.95 ;
·  V(s_A) = 5.0 ,  V(s_B) = 4.5 .

Continuation gap:

\Delta V_{\text{cont}} = 0.95 \cdot (5.0 - 4.5) = 0.475.

Dynamic switching condition for  A \to B :

\Delta Q \le \lambda C_{AB} + \Delta V_{\text{cont}} = 0.05 + 0.475 = 0.525.

Since  \Delta Q = 0.10 < 0.525 , the system stays in  s_A  even though  s_B  has higher immediate utility. The reason:  s_A  offers higher future value through better accessibility to high-utility topologies.

