Appendix E. Constrained Reconfiguration — Full Details

E.1 Peak Constraints via Edge Pruning

Proposition E.1. Let the constraint be  c_T(s,s') \le C_{\max}  for every elementary transition. Then the constrained reconfiguration problem is equivalent to the unconstrained problem on the pruned graph  \mathfrak{R}_T' = (\mathcal{S}_{\text{feas}}, \mathcal{E}_T')  where

\mathcal{E}_T' = \{(s,s') \in \mathcal{E}_T : c_T(s,s') \le C_{\max}\}.

Proof. Any trajectory satisfying the peak constraint uses only edges with  c_T \le C_{\max} , hence lies in  \mathfrak{R}_T' . Conversely, any trajectory in  \mathfrak{R}_T'  satisfies the peak constraint.  \square 

E.2 Expected-Cost Constraints via Lagrangian

Theorem E.2. Under Slater's condition, the expected-cost constrained problem

\max_\pi \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_t \gamma^t Q(s_t, T_t) \right] \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_t \gamma^t c_T(s_t, s_{t+1}) \right] \le C_{\max}

has zero duality gap, and the optimal value equals

\min_{\mu \ge 0} \max_\pi \mathbb{E}\left[ \sum_t \gamma^t (Q(s_t, T_t) - \mu c_T(s_t, s_{t+1})) \right] + \mu C_{\max}.

Proof. This is the standard Lagrangian duality theorem for convex programs, applied to the state-occupation measure LP formulation of the CMDP.  \square 

E.3 Peak-Cost Duality Gap

Proposition E.3. There exist peak-cost constrained MDPs with a finite duality gap even in two-state models.

Proof (counterexample sketch). Consider a two-state MDP with states  \{a, b\} , actions  \{L, R\} , deterministic transitions. Let the peak constraint be "cost  \le 1  in every step." Construct rewards such that the optimal feasible policy uses a mixed strategy, but the Lagrangian relaxation admits an infeasible deterministic policy with higher Lagrangian value. The gap arises because the peak constraint is not convex in the occupation measure.  \square 

