Appendix F. Learning Theory Details

F.1 Pseudo-Dimension and Uniform Convergence

Definition F.1 (Pseudo-dimension). Let  \mathcal{F}  be a class of real-valued functions on a domain  \mathcal{Z} . The pseudo-dimension  d_{\mathcal{F}}  is the largest  n  such that there exist  z_1, \dots, z_n \in \mathcal{Z}  and thresholds  t_1, \dots, t_n \in \mathbb{R}  such that for every binary labeling  (b_1, \dots, b_n) \in \{0,1\}^n , there exists  f \in \mathcal{F}  with

f(z_i) > t_i \iff b_i = 1.

Theorem F.2 (Uniform convergence). Let  \mathcal{F}: \mathcal{Z} \to [0, C_{\max}]  have pseudo-dimension  d_{\mathcal{F}} . For any  \epsilon, \delta > 0 , if

N \ge \mathcal{O}\left( \frac{C_{\max}^2}{\epsilon^2} \left[ d_{\mathcal{F}} \log\left(\frac{C_{\max}}{\epsilon}\right) + \log\left(\frac{1}{\delta}\right) \right] \right),

then with probability at least  1 - \delta  over i.i.d. samples  z_1, \dots, z_N ,

\sup_{f \in \mathcal{F}} \left| \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N f(z_i) - \mathbb{E}[f] \right| \le \epsilon.

Proof. Standard result via chaining or VC-theoretic arguments.  \square 

F.2 Decision-Preserving Bound

Theorem F.3. Under the conditions of Theorem F.2 with  \mathcal{Z} = \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{S} \times \mathcal{T}  and  f(z) = |\widehat{C}_\theta(s,s',T) - c| , if  \|\widehat{C}_\theta - C_T\|_\infty \le \epsilon  uniformly, then for any pair  (s,s')  with

|\Delta Q_T(s,s') - \lambda C_T(s,s')| > \lambda \epsilon,

the decision under  \widehat{C}_\theta  equals the exact optimal decision.

Proof. By Theorem B.11, the decision can only differ if  |\Delta Q_T - \lambda C_T| \le \lambda \epsilon . The contrapositive gives the stated result.  \square 

F.3 Online Regret with Switching Costs

Theorem F.4 (Dekel et al., 2014). For adversarial bandits with switching costs, the minimax regret over  T  rounds is  \widetilde{\Theta}(T^{2/3}) .

Corollary F.5. In the online reconfiguration setting with adversarial task sequences and nonzero switching costs, any online policy has regret at least  \Omega(T^{2/3}) , and this bound is achievable up to logarithmic factors.

