Appendix G. Counterexamples and Edge Cases

G.1 Asymmetry of  C_T 

Example G.1. Let  G  use model  M_1  with a small initialization cost but no cached state; let  G'  use model  M_2  with an expensive initialization but pre-loaded context.

Transition  G \to G' : expensive initialization of  M_2  plus context transfer:  c_T(G, G') = 0.20 .

Transition  G' \to G : cheap, because  M_1 's context is fully serializable and can be re-loaded quickly:  c_T(G', G) = 0.05 .

Hence  C_T(G, G') = 0.20 \ne 0.05 = C_T(G', G) .

G.2 Failure of Identity of Indiscernibles

Example G.2. Let  G  and  G'  differ only in the ordering of two independent parallel branches. Since the branches are independent, the routing semantics  \rho  is identical, and  c_T(G, G') = c_T(G', G) = 0  because no re-initialization is needed. But  G \ne G' .

G.3 Failure of Concatenation under History-Dependent Admissibility

Example G.3. Let admissibility depend on a budget  b_t  consumed at each step. Suppose the budget is 10. Trajectory  \Gamma_1: s_0 \to s_1  consumes 6, leaving 4. Trajectory  \Gamma_2: s_1 \to s_2  consumes 5, exceeding the remaining 4. The concatenation  \Gamma_1 \circ \Gamma_2  is infeasible even though  \Gamma_1  and  \Gamma_2  are separately feasible from the standpoint of the original budget.

